[s0l0!-header][h1]The For Loop[/h1][/s0l0!-header] 

Now we've got the theory, let's look at a specific example.

This example creates a [b]for [/b]loop that prints numbers 1 through 5:.[code format="js" codeId="4866"]for (i=1; i<=5; i++) {
   document.write(i + "<br />");
}[/code]
So what's actually happening? 

In this example, [b]Statement 1[/b] sets a variable before the loop starts (var i = 1).
[b]Statement 2[/b] defines the condition for the loop to run (it must be less than or equal to 5).
[b]Statement 3[/b] increases a value (i++) each time the code block in the loop has been executed.

[s0l0!-header][b]Result:[/b][/s0l0!-header] [img id="2755" width="100%"]
[b]Statement 1[/b] is optional, and can be left out, if your values are set before the loop starts.[code format="js" codeId="4867"]var i = 1;
for (; i<=5; i++) {
   document.write(i + "<br />");
}[/code]
You can also initiate more than one value in [b]statement 1[/b], using [b]commas [/b]to separate them.[code format="js" codeId="4868"]for ([b]i=1, text=""[/b]; i<=5; i++) {
   text = i;
   document.write(i + "<br />");
}[/code]
[note][b]Heads up![/b]
ES6 introduces other for loop types; you can learn about them in the ES6 course later.[/note]
